        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "During the First World War, Britain made conflicting diplomatic promises that sowed long-term distrust. In the 1915 McMahon-Hussein Correspondence, Britain promised Arab independence in exchange for an uprising against the Ottoman Empire. However, in 1916 Britain secretly signed the Sykes-Picot Agreement with France, dividing Ottoman territories into Anglo-French zones of influence. Tensions worsened when Britain issued the 1917 Balfour Declaration, supporting a Jewish national home in Palestine.<br><br>Following the Allied victory in 1918, these irreconcilable pledges triggered immediate friction. When the League of Nations granted Britain the Mandate for Palestine in 1922, Palestinian Arabs felt deeply betrayed by the denial of self-determination. Violent inter-communal clashes erupted, including the 1920 Jerusalem riots and 1921 Jaffa riots.<br><br>Finally, Britain attempted to manage rising violence through the 1922 Churchill White Paper, reassuring Arabs that Palestine would not become 'as Jewish as England is English'. However, this satisfied neither community and entrenched permanent sectarian hostility under British rule.",
