        "modelAnswer": "The Golan Heights was of paramount importance for Israeli security because its elevated geography completely dominated the valleys below. Between 1948 and 1967, Syrian artillery gunners had occupied the high escarpment of the Golan, repeatedly shelling Israeli farming kibbutzim in Upper Galilee and targeting tractors in the Demilitarized Zone. By capturing the heights in June 1967, the IDF eliminated this permanent bombardment threat and established an impenetrable defensive buffer.<br><br>Furthermore, controlling the Golan was vital for securing Israel’s national water supply and strategic defence. The heights contained the headwaters of the Jordan River and Lake Kinneret (Sea of Galilee), thwarting Syrian engineering plans to divert freshwater away from Israel. Additionally, occupying the plateau placed Israeli tanks just forty miles from Damascus, giving Israel immense strategic deterrence against future Syrian aggression.",
